01:34:52.219 --> 01:34:53.219
Alright cutting myself off cause i've always
wanted to explain this thingy Mangaka’s

01:34:53.219 --> 01:34:54.219
do.

01:34:54.219 --> 01:34:55.219
Gege does it occasionally from what i've seen
and Yuki did it here.

01:34:55.219 --> 01:34:56.219
Its called “author's fiat" or "authorial
fiat."

01:34:56.219 --> 01:34:57.219
It refers to a situation in storytelling,
particularly in manga, where the author introduces

01:34:57.219 --> 01:34:58.219
a plot development or outcome simply because
they have stated it to be so, without providing

01:34:58.219 --> 01:34:59.219
a logical or organic explanation within the
narrative itself.

01:34:59.219 --> 01:35:00.219
Its often used to resolve complex or seemingly
unsolvable plot points, establish new rules

01:35:00.219 --> 01:35:01.219
or abilities, or make significant changes
to the story without a clear build-up or explanation.

01:35:01.219 --> 01:35:02.219
Some people think it can take away from the
story, but tbh I like it if its used correctly

01:35:02.219 --> 01:35:03.219
as it can truly add to the hype of what's
to come.

01:35:03.219 --> 01:35:04.219
I’d love to know your thoughts on it though,
so be sure to let me know down below!

01:35:04.219 --> 01:35:05.219
Anyways back to the story!

01:35:05.219 --> 01:35:06.219
Obviously Gabimaru was super glad to see that
Sagiri is okay since he reminds her that he

01:35:06.219 --> 01:35:07.219
needs her in order to return and receive pardon.

01:35:07.219 --> 01:35:08.219
Despite everyone feeling exhausted, Chōbei
says that they have to fight back no matter

01:35:08.219 --> 01:35:09.219
what happens.

01:35:09.219 --> 01:35:10.219
He then walks up to Gabimaru and complements
him on their first battle before stating that

01:35:10.219 --> 01:35:12.690
given what he’s seen its fairly evident
that he’s also got something to lose.

01:35:12.690 --> 01:35:17.510
